question_fine_print:
* The current law alternatively sunsets when greenhouse gas emissions of the United States are less than or equal to 25% of 2022 emissions. This question concerns the scheduled year; if the greenhouse gas emission threshold changes but the scheduled sunset year remains 2032 then it will not count, even if the stated purpose or effective outcome is an earlier phasing out of 48E.
* For the purposes of this question, a safe harbor must grandfather in most projects that are ongoing at the time of enactment.
* The question will resolve immediately once the criteria for any outcome are met, even if later developments would satisfy the criteria of another outcome.&#x20;

question_description:
The [Clean Electricity Investment Tax Credit](https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/clean-electricity-investment-credit), also known as 48E due to it being in that section of the tax code, is an investment tax credit (ITC) for companies that invest in zero-greenhouse gas energy generation (such as solar and nuclear), storage (e.g., [Tesla Megapacks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesla_Megapack)) and any related equipment needed. The credit has a base of 6% of the investment, which can rise up to 30% if certain wage and apprenticeship requirements are met, and up to 50% if additional domestic content and location requirements are met.&#x20;

The 48E tax credit was part of the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act and took effect for new zero-GHG facilities beginning construction after December 31, 2025. (For more information, see Columbia University's [Inflation Reduction Act Tracker](https://iratracker.org/programs/ira-section-13102-renewable-energy-investment-tax-credit/).) 48E is currently scheduled to phase out at the later of either 2032 or when United States greenhouse gas emissions from electricity are less than or equal to 25% of 2022 emissions.

With the November 2024 election of Donald Trump and Republican majorities in both the Senate and House of Representatives, the future of 48E, along with other clean energy tax credits, has been thrown into uncertainty. Under Senate budget reconciliation rules, spending programs such as 48E can be modified or eliminated through a simple majority vote, without being subject to filibuster from Democratic senators.&#x20;

According to the [Brookings Institution](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-will-happen-to-the-inflation-reduction-act-under-a-republican-trifecta/):&#x20;

> Republican lawmakers have cited the cost of the IRA as one of the reasons to roll it back—for example, the House Committee on Ways and Means has [highlighted](https://gop-waysandmeans.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/230421-Two-Pager-Green-Energy-Credits-FINAL.pdf) the “exploding” cost of “green corporate welfare” under the IRA. The [cost of IRA’s “green subsidies”](https://waysandmeans.house.gov/2023/04/26/house-republicans-pass-limit-save-grow-act-to-rein-in-wasteful-spending-and-grow-the-economy/) was a part of the justification for introducing the [Limit, Save, Grow Act of 2023](https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/2811), that [sought](https://climatepower.us/research-polling/inflation-reduction-act-repeal-votes-tracker/) to cut billions in funding from climate, environmental justice, and clean energy tax incentives from the IRA.

> Previous House attempts to roll back the IRA may provide an indication of the specific provisions that could be targeted under a Republican White House and Congress. According to Climate Power’s [IRA repeal votes tracker](https://climatepower.us/research-polling/inflation-reduction-act-repeal-votes-tracker/), as of October 15, 2024, the attempts to roll back the IRA have repeatedly focused on preventing funding for Executive Order 14082 (which helps implement the IRA), repealing sections related to the Greenhouse Gas Reduction Fund, and repealing sections related to the Methane Emissions Reduction Program.

Some conservatives have advocated for repealing clean energy tax credits such as 48E as soon as possible, while others [have advocated for](https://www.eenews.net/articles/republicans-mull-thoughtful-phaseout-of-green-credits/) a more "thoughtful" approach, using earlier phase-outs rather than quick repeal, in order to provide companies with some visibility in their project planning. Republicans hold a slim majority of 5 seats in the House, and many representatives come from districts that benefit from renewable energy investments and are facing re-election battles in 2026.&#x20;

According to one expert forecast from the 600-attorney corporate law firm of Crowell & Moring, 48E is considered a "[moderate](https://www.crowell.com/en/insights/client-alerts/clean-energy-tax-credits-and-after-the-election-what-to-expect)" risk of repeal, due to the perceived cost of the tax credit by Republican lawmakers. However, other options rather than changing the sunset date could be removing the so-called adders such as the wage and apprenticeship requirements..
